# Entity Conventions

## Class Naming

Entity classes must be suffixed with `Entity` (e.g. `CharacterEntity`, `ChatEntity`). Never use the bare domain name as the class name.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
export class Character { ... }

// ✅ GOOD
export class CharacterEntity { ... }
```

The `Entity` suffix also carries through to input types: `CreateCharacterEntityInput`, `UpdateCharacterEntityInput`.

## Primary Key

Always use `@PrimaryGeneratedColumn('uuid')` with type `string`. Never use auto-increment integer ids.

## Required Timestamp Fields

Every entity must include `createdAt` and `updatedAt` fields using TypeORM's `@CreateDateColumn` and `@UpdateDateColumn` decorators with `type: 'timestamptz'` to preserve timezone information.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — integer id, missing timestamp fields
@Entity('characters')
export class CharacterEntity {
  @PrimaryGeneratedColumn()
  id: number;

  @Column({ length: 255 })
  name: string;
}

// ✅ GOOD
import { Column, CreateDateColumn, Entity, PrimaryGeneratedColumn, UpdateDateColumn } from 'typeorm';

@Entity('characters')
export class CharacterEntity {
  @PrimaryGeneratedColumn('uuid')
  id: string;

  @Column({ length: 255 })
  name: string;

  @CreateDateColumn({ type: 'timestamptz' })
  createdAt: Date;

  @UpdateDateColumn({ type: 'timestamptz' })
  updatedAt: Date;
}
```

## Input Types

Every entity file must export a `Create<Name>EntityInput` and `Update<Name>EntityInput` type at the bottom of the file, after the entity class. `CreateInput` omits auto-generated fields (`id`, `createdAt`, `updatedAt`). `UpdateInput` omits only timestamps (`createdAt`, `updatedAt`), makes the remaining fields optional via `Partial`, and always requires `id`.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — no input types exported from entity file

// ❌ BAD — input types defined in a separate file or DTO
// character.dto.ts
export type CreateCharacterEntityInput = { ... };

// ✅ GOOD — at the bottom of character.entity.ts
export type CreateCharacterEntityInput = Omit<
  CharacterEntity,
  'id' | 'createdAt' | 'updatedAt'
>;
export type UpdateCharacterEntityInput = Partial<
  Omit<CharacterEntity, 'createdAt' | 'updatedAt'>
> & { id: string };
```

## Enums

Use TypeScript `enum` for columns with a fixed set of values. Place all enums for an entity in a single `<entity>.enum.ts` file alongside the entity and import them. Reference the enum in both the `@Column` decorator and the property type.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — inline string union
@Column({ type: 'enum', enum: ['male', 'female', 'non_binary'] })
sex: 'male' | 'female' | 'non_binary';

// ❌ BAD — enum defined inside the entity file
// character.entity.ts
export enum Sex { Male = 'male' }
@Entity('characters')
export class CharacterEntity { ... }

// ✅ GOOD — enum in a separate file
// character.enum.ts
export enum Sex {
  Male = 'male',
  Female = 'female',
  NonBinary = 'non_binary',
}

// character.entity.ts
import { Sex } from './character.enum';

@Column({ type: 'enum', enum: Sex })
sex: Sex;
```
